## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical, beginner-friendly observability implementation: instrumenting a new service end-to-end with minimal changes and validating traces in a production UI.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry auto-instrumentation
- Trace-context propagation and log correlation
- Lightweight instrumentation for HTTP and database spans
- OTLP exporter configuration and backend dashboard validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Node example: enable auto-instrumentation
// Install: @opentelemetry/api @opentelemetry/sdk-node @opentelemetry/instrumentation-http
import { NodeSDK } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-node';
import { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } from '@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node';

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
  serviceName: 'accounts-service',
  traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter()
});
sdk.start();

// Trace ID in logs
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const traceId = trace.getActiveSpan()?.spanContext().traceId;
  console.log(`Request ${req.method} ${req.path} [trace=${traceId}]`);
  next();
});
```

## Validation Plan
- Configure OTLP exporter to your backend (Jaeger/Tempo/Grafana)
- Verify HTTP request spans appear in dashboard
- Confirm database query spans are captured
- Check trace IDs appear in structured logs
- Test end-to-end with synthetic request and validate trace flow

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical SRE skills: incident triage, region-aware rollouts, backpressure, circuit breaking, and rollback discipline beyond basic incident response.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region latency analysis
- Cache stampede mitigation
- Per-region deployment strategies (canary, rollouts)
- Safe rollback criteria and instrumentation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple circuit breaker (pseudo)
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold) { this.open = false; this.failures = 0; this.threshold = threshold; }
  onFailure() { this.failures++; if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.open = true; }
  allow() { return !this.open; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to a service mesh with traffic shifting?
- How would you verify rollback correctness across all regions post-incident?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to diagnose rate-limiter issues, design clear observability, and execute a controlled rollback with minimal disruption.

## Key Concepts
- Token bucket rate limiting across regions
- Observability for distributed systems
- Canary testing and safe traffic shifts
- Rollback and validation under time pressure
- Post-incident analysis and preventive fixes

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo token bucket core (simplified)
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) { this.rate = rate; this.capacity = capacity; this.tokens = capacity; this.last = Date.now(); }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const delta = (now - this.last) * this.rate / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + delta);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) { this.tokens -= 1; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design robust global traffic shaping, fault isolation, and controlled rollback mechanisms under partial-region degradation.

## Key Concepts
- Per-region circuit breakers and quotas
- Regional backpressure and buffering
- Canary testing and safe rollback
- Metrics: p95 latency, error rate, saturation, queue depth

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sketch of per-region breaker state
class RegionalBreaker {
  constructor(threshold, windowMs = 60000) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.window = windowMs;
    this.failures = 0;
    this.successes = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
    this.lastFailureTime = 0;
  }

  recordSuccess() {
    this.successes++;
    if (this.state === 'HALF_OPEN' && this.successes > this.threshold) {
      this.state = 'CLOSED';
      this.failures = 0;
      this.successes = 0;
    }
  }

  recordFailure() {
    this.failures++;
    this.lastFailureTime = Date.now();
    const errorRate = this.failures / (this.failures + this.successes);
    if (errorRate >= this.threshold) {
      this.state = 'OPEN';
    }
  }

  canAttemptRequest() {
    if (this.state === 'OPEN') {
      const timeSinceFailure = Date.now() - this.lastFailureTime;
      if (timeSinceFailure > this.window) {
        this.state = 'HALF_OPEN';
        return true;
      }
      return false;
    }
    return true;
  }
}

// Token bucket quota per region
class RegionalQuota {
  constructor(maxTokens, refillRate) {
    this.tokens = maxTokens;
    this.maxTokens = maxTokens;
    this.refillRate = refillRate;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }

  tryConsume(tokens = 1) {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= tokens) {
      this.tokens -= tokens;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const tokensToAdd = (now - this.lastRefill) * this.refillRate / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.maxTokens, this.tokens + tokensToAdd);
    this.lastRefill = now;
  }
}
```

This implementation provides:
- **Circuit Breaker**: Tracks failure rates and transitions between CLOSED, OPEN, and HALF_OPEN states
- **Token Bucket Quota**: Enforces rate limits per region with configurable refill rates
- **Observable Metrics**: Both classes support instrumentation for monitoring p95 latency, error rates, and quota utilization
